From 6eab7fbb582962a62642b348913e57f2de2642e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Borkmann <daniel@iogearbox.net>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 11:28:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: build without LLVM's GlobalMerge pass

LLVM 20 runs the GlobalMerge pass on RISC-V by default.  It folds
static globals that are used together into one .L_MergedGlobals object
and keeps the original names as aliases into it, per-CPU variables in
.data..percpu included.  pahole 1.25 then emits a VAR for the alias and
for the merged object at the same offset, the .data..percpu DATASEC of
vmlinux gets overlapping entries, and the kernel rejects vmlinux BTF at
boot:

  BPF: type_id=70575 offset=4224 size=8
  BPF: Invalid offset

Without vmlinux BTF every fentry, fexit, fmod_ret, lsm and iterator
program fails to load with -EINVAL, and so does every module that
carries BTF ("failed to validate module [dummy] BTF: -22").

clang 18 does not merge these globals, and arm64 and x86 builds with
clang 20 are not affected.  Turn the pass off for RISC-V with the
backend option, which LLVM 18 accepts as well; cc-option drops it for
compilers that do not know it.
---
 arch/riscv/Makefile | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/Makefile b/arch/riscv/Makefile
index 44f5a439741a9..c5c7e909b9cdd 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/Makefile
+++ b/arch/riscv/Makefile
@@ -109,6 +109,12 @@ KBUILD_AFLAGS += $(call as-option,-Wa$(comma)-mno-arch-attr)
 KBUILD_CFLAGS_MODULE += $(call cc-option,-mno-relax)
 KBUILD_AFLAGS_MODULE += $(call as-option,-Wa$(comma)-mno-relax)
 
+# LLVM 20 runs GlobalMerge on RISC-V by default and folds static globals,
+# per-CPU ones included, into .L_MergedGlobals with the old names left as
+# aliases.  pahole then describes both at the same .data..percpu offset and
+# the kernel rejects vmlinux BTF as invalid.
+KBUILD_CFLAGS += $(call cc-option,-mllvm -riscv-enable-global-merge=false)
+
 # GCC versions that support the "-mstrict-align" option default to allowing
 # unaligned accesses.  While unaligned accesses are explicitly allowed in the
 # RISC-V ISA, they're emulated by machine mode traps on all extant
